<title>SINGAPORE: Cargill's N.Korea wheat/zinc barter delayed - trade.</title>
<headline>Cargill's N.Korea wheat/zinc barter delayed - trade.</headline>
<dateline>SINGAPORE 1997-06-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. trader Cargill's shipment of 20,000 tonnes of wheat for North Korea has been delayed by uncertainy over the delivery of 4,000 tonnes of zinc from the Korean side in the barter deal, trade sources said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>In early April, Cargill and hunger-stricken North Korea agreed to swap the wheat for zinc, with each parcel of the two commodities worth about $4 million.</p>
<p>&quot;The wheat was set to arrive on June 1 or 2 at North Korea's western port of Nampo. But the ship is on the open sea,&quot; one Asian grain trading source said.</p>
<p>Cargill declined comment on the issue.  </p>
<p>The zinc was not ready for shipment before the arrival of the wheat vessel, as specified in the barter deal agreement, and Cargill is in negotiations with the North Koreans to avoid the transaction being aborted, another source said.</p>
<p>Cargill and North Korea had been discussing the wheat sale since January following the U.S. government's clearance for the sale of up to 500,000 tonnes of wheat and/or rice to the communist country.</p>
</text>
